{
  "dom_id": "step-q42864",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is  $\\text{ }-\\frac{1}{11}+(-\\frac{3}{11})?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we drop the parentheses and the addition sign, which gives\n\n$$\n\\text{ }-\\frac{1}{11}+(-\\frac{3}{11})=-\\frac{1}{11}-\\frac{3}{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since both fractions have a common denominator of $11$, we can combine the numerators, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{11}-\\frac{3}{11} & =\\frac{-1-3}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $-1-3$ using our number line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14149424/assets/64d008419fd882c918e73b577956b3e36d3a32814fff62d495c0f75b7d107cd1.png)\n\nWe find that $-1-3=-4.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-1-3}{11}=\\frac{-4}{11}=-\\frac{4}{11}.\n$$",
